    "turn_idx": 332,
    "turn_text": "It's the stories. I mean, listen, when I was at Hopkins, I didn't mind Masters in Public Health, Masters in Nursing, to grant John Hopkins. I was the youngest member of my class there. I had a good girlfriend. Dr. Shalon Irving, when she enrolled in the MPH program, she already had a dual doctorate in gerontology and sociology. She was brilliant. She was beautiful. And we were just good friends. Well, fast forward, like 10 years. And when I was wrapping up my service in the Obama administration, she was getting ready to have her first child. And she was so excited. It is a beautiful baby girl named Soleil. And then three weeks after she gave birth, We lost her. She died. And it was devastating. Shalom was a lieutenant commander in the United States Public Health Service Commission Corps. She had focused her life and her work as an epidemiologist on ending health disparities. And she was working at the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. I will never forget just being devastated at her funeral and the CDC director came. and spoke at her funeral and was talking about how we know the disparities and someone who is like an expert in this area still dies because of all of these issues that we talk about. Now, Shalom's story has been well documented by ProPublica actually at this great article series that NPR, I think, picked up about her life and death. And now her mother, Ms. Wanda, has started an organization called Dr. Shalom's Map. that folks should look up if they like, but it took something that was, you know, like a clinical fact and made it really personal to me. And then, you know, it just emphasized how much I was not alone. I feel like all of us. and know somebody who's been impacted in this way. And I knew that if I were elected to Congress, this is something",
